From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Blake <chrisrblake93@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 10:09:12 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] board: rockchip: add Ulanzi D200

Add U-Boot support for the Ulanzi D200 (RK3308B-S), with its own board
target and config header.

- SPL loads U-Boot from SPI NAND (raw "u-boot" partition, falling back
  to "u-boot-backup"). SFC DMA is disabled in SPL since it cannot write
  to SRAM, where part of TF-A is loaded.
- If SPL cannot boot anything it enters maskrom mode, as it does on
  Ctrl-B on the console during power on; the 'maskrom' command does
  the same from U-Boot.
- The DesignWare watchdog may already be running when U-Boot starts
  (USB boot path, ~21 s), so WDT support is enabled to service it.
- serial# and cpuid# are derived from the CPU ID in OTP (MISC_INIT_R).
- Display: RK3308 VOP + command-sequence panel + PWM backlight,
  with the VOP bound before relocation so the framebuffer is reserved.
- The console defaults to the UART only: the panel sits behind the keys,
  so only the boot logo is shown on it. The board code probes the
  display itself, since the console no longer does.
- Optional user splash: if the "ubi" partition has a volume named
  "splash" holding a BMP, it is shown centred instead of the built-in
  logo; anything missing or invalid keeps the built-in logo.
- The environment lives in the "ubi" partition (redundant volumes
  env/env_r, created on first saveenv, fastmap enabled).
- Fastboot over USB (own product ID 0x0006, 32 MiB buffer at
  0x02000000 to fit the 128 MiB of RAM, 'oem run' and 'oem console'),
  flashing the SPI NAND partitions and the UBI volumes by name.
- Linux is booted from the UBI volume "kernel", holding a FIT image
  (kernel + device tree): ubi read + bootm. The kernel's built-in
  command line is used unless "bootargs" is set.
- preboot is enabled, so a reboot into the fastboot boot mode (e.g.
  "reboot fastboot" with the Linux syscon reboot-mode driver) starts
  fastboot.
- A maskrom USB boot image is built for development
  (u-boot-rockchip-usb471.bin / usb472.bin).

Signed-off-by: Chris Blake <chrisrblake93@gmail.com>
